.TH cuundo n 0.5 ctrlutils
.SH ctrlutils::cuundo
.PP
A controller that provides undo/redo for the file operations \fBcufileops\fR performs. cufileops emits an operation record after each successful op (through its \fB\-onop\fR callback); cuundo records those on an undo stack and reverses them on request, with a redo stack so a reversal can itself be redone. The two stay decoupled \-\- cufileops does not know cuundo exists, it just reports what it did.
.SS API
.CS
package require ctrlutils::cuundo

::ctrlutils::cuundo::install $f $prov ?-list widget? ?-refresh script?
::ctrlutils::cuundo::record  $f $recordDict     ;# the -onop target
::ctrlutils::cuundo::undo    $f
::ctrlutils::cuundo::redo    $f
::ctrlutils::cuundo::canUndo $f                 ;# / canRedo
::ctrlutils::cuundo::clear   $f
.CE
.IP \(bu
\fBinstall $f $prov\fR \-\- set up undo/redo state for the view \fB$f\fR, reversing through \fB$prov\fR. \fB\-list widget\fR binds Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y (and Ctrl+Shift+Z) on the list body. An unknown option is an error listing the known ones, \fB{CTRLUTILS CUUNDO OPTION <opt>}\fR (0.2; up to 0.1 a typo like \fB\-lst\fR silently lost the key bindings). \fB\-refresh script\fR runs after each undo and redo; without it cuundo calls \fBcufileops::refresh $f\fR, the refresh script given to cufileops for the same view. A failing refresh is an error \fB{CTRLUTILS CUUNDO REFRESH}\fR; the step itself is done (0.5; up to 0.4 cuundo called the private \fBcufileops::_refresh\fR inside \fBcatch\fR).
.IP \(bu
\fBrecord $f $rec\fR \-\- record an operation. Pass it to cufileops as \fB\-onop [list ctrlutils::cuundo::record $f]\fR. New activity clears the redo stack.
.IP \(bu
\fBundo\fR / \fBredo\fR \-\- reverse / re\-apply the last operation.
.IP \(bu
\fBcanUndo\fR / \fBcanRedo\fR \-\- whether a stack is non\-empty (for enabling menu items).
.IP \(bu
\fBclear\fR \-\- drop both stacks.
.SS "What is reversed"
.PP
Each operation is reversed through the provider:
.TS
l l.
Operation	Undo
rename from\->to	move to\->from
mkdir path	delete path
paste path	delete path (the newly created target)
delete path	restore from a snapshot taken \fIbefore\fR deleting
.TE
.PP
For delete, cufileops captures the content before deleting (a file's bytes, or a whole directory subtree), and cuundo restores it. Reversal of a delete is only possible when the provider can read the content back; a snapshot is held in memory, so this is best suited to ordinary file sizes.
.SS Wiring
.CS
::ctrlutils::cuundo::install $f $prov -list $list
::ctrlutils::cufileops::install $f $prov $list $refresh \
    -onop [list ::ctrlutils::cuundo::record $f]
.CE
.SS "See also"
.PP
\fBcufileops\fR, \fBcumonitor\fR, \fBcufilter\fR, \fBtclutils::tuprovider\fR
